B.Rq=new A.FA(u.cA,"/encryption/journey/key-custody","Follow the key",null)
B.Rr=new A.f0("Stop making memory do the wrong job","A person is good at remembering a small number of meaningful things. A machine is good at generating and storing many unrelated strings. A password manager moves the repetitive job to the machine: one unique generated credential per service, retrieved only when that service is being used.",null)
B.Rt=new A.FB("Three different roles","Plaintext is the readable original. An encryption algorithm combines it with a key to produce ciphertext. Decryption uses the appropriate key to recover the plaintext. Security should depend on protecting the key\u2014not on hiding how the algorithm works.",null)
B.Ru=new A.f0("You are choosing where to spend the first five guesses","A stolen database may let an attacker make billions of guesses, but order still matters. A match in the first hundred costs almost nothing; a match after an enormous search may cost more than the account is worth. Rank these candidates from the one you think an attacker tries first to the one they leave latest.",null)
B.Rv=new A.f0("Not every second factor makes the same promise","An authenticator code proves that someone obtained a short-lived code, but a convincing fake site can ask the user to relay it. A security key signs for the real site\u2019s identity, so the factor itself can refuse the lookalike. MFA adds another condition; phishing-resistant MFA also binds that condition to the right site.",null)
B.Rw=new A.f0("Length and complexity answer a narrower question","Counting characters estimates the size of an exhaustive search only when those characters were chosen independently and at random. People rarely choose that way. A long famous phrase or a season followed by the current year occupies one very likely spot in an attacker\u2019s list\u2014not a random spot in a vast space.\n\nThat is why \u201cSummer2026!\u201d can fall before a shorter random password: the attacker is modelling people before searching possibilities.",null)
B.Rx=new A.f0("By guessing \u2014 and it matters enormously where from","Nobody can read your password back, so nobody tries to. They try candidates instead, and see whether one is accepted.\n\nThat sounds hopeless, and at a login page it very nearly is. But guessing does not only happen at login pages, and where an attacker is standing when they guess changes the answer more than anything you could do to the password itself.",null)
B.Ry=new A.f0("CAPTCHA, briefly","CAPTCHA stands for \u201cCompletely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart.\u201d It is a challenge meant to distinguish a person from an automated program, often called a bot. It is the same idea as a lockout, aimed at a different part of the problem: it does not limit how many guesses are allowed, it makes each one cost a human moment.\n\nLike a lockout, it does not make any password stronger. It makes automation more expensive.",null)
B.RA=new A.f0("It does not stay on your machine","You type it, and it travels. It leaves your keyboard, crosses a network you do not control, and arrives at a service you have never seen. Every one of those places is somewhere it could, in principle, be observed.\n\nThat much most people expect. The next part is where intuition usually goes wrong.",null)
B.RB=new A.f0("Because somebody is counting","A service that answers guesses can also refuse to. After a few failures it can pause, then pause for longer, then stop answering that account at all until its owner proves who they are. It can slow down a whole network address rather than one account.\n\nNone of that requires knowing whether the password was any good. It works on the attempt, not on the secret.",null)
B.RE=new A.f0("Yes \u2014 and that is the whole reason databases get stolen","Take the file the service compares against, and every defence from the last step stops applying. No lockout, because nobody is being asked. No CAPTCHA, because nothing is being submitted. The attacker is now working entirely on their own machines.\n\nSo what did they get? Not your password. Something derived from it. Here is what that looks like.",null)
B.RF=new A.f0("The rates are assumptions, not constants","Nobody has measured \u201cten billion guesses per second\u201d for your account. The real figure depends on hardware, and far more on how the passwords were stored \u2014 a choice made by the service, long before anyone attacked it.\n\nWhat the numbers are for is the gap between them. Seconds against centuries is the finding. Four decimal places would be a pretence.",null)
B.RH=new A.f0("The method is the evidence","A password does not become random because it looks messy. If a person chose the word, capitalised the first letter, added the year and ended with an exclamation mark, an attacker can model that process. If a generator made independent random choices, the attacker has no shorter route than searching those choices.",null)
B.Rs=new A.f0("What MFA means","Multi-factor authentication (MFA) asks for evidence from two or more different categories. A password is something you know. A phone, authenticator app, security key, or registered device is something you have. A fingerprint or face is something you are. A password plus a PIN is still one kind of evidence; adding a code from a phone introduces another.",null)
B.ZV=new A.TH(null)
B.z=new A.h(null,24,null,null)
B.RG=new A.f0("Let the system answer","In both runs below, the attacker already has a password that the authentication engine accepts. Change only the device the attempt comes from, then compare the chain of consequences. This is the same executable Password Security model used in Systems Studio\u2014not a scripted illustration.",null)
B.ZW=new A.Gb(null)
B.Xg=s([B.Rs,B.ZV,B.z,B.RG,B.ZW],t.p)
B.RI=new A.on(8,"A password is not the last word",u.m,B.Xg,u.l,"/password/journey/security-key","Test which factor recognises the site",null)
B.RD=new A.f0("Build a system, not a silver bullet","Choose protections, then move between the attacks from this journey. A useful layer might prevent an attack, make it less effective, or reveal it in time to respond. It may also do nothing at all against a different path. The goal is coverage with overlapping jobs\u2014not one control asked to solve every problem.",null)
B.Ms=new A.Eb(null)
B.a2F=new A.dO("LinkedIn credentials and the Dropbox intrusion","Credentials exposed in the LinkedIn breach were later reused to access a Dropbox employee account, showing how one organization\u2019s breach can become another organization\u2019s entry point.","The intrusion exposed user information and forced a security response extending beyond the service where the original password was lost.","Unique passwords bound the damage of a breached credential. Monitoring, MFA, and restricted employee access add layers when reuse still occurs.")
B.a2O=new A.dO("The Adobe password breach","Attackers obtained a large collection of encrypted passwords together with password hints, and repeated encrypted values revealed patterns among accounts.","Millions of credentials required resets, while predictable passwords and reuse created risk on other services as well as Adobe accounts.","Credential storage must use slow, salted password hashing, but storage design cannot compensate for reused passwords, weak recovery, or missing detection.")
B.Xx=s([B.a2F,B.a2O],t.AU)
B.CT=new A.hU("password","Account incidents repeatedly show that password choice, password storage, reuse, MFA, recovery, and monitoring form one connected system.",B.Xx,null)
B.Uo=s([B.RD,B.Ms,B.CT],t.p)
B.RJ=new A.on(11,"No single perfect control",u.B,B.Uo,"What have these failures looked like in real organizations, and what were the consequences?","/password/real-world","Review real-world outcomes",null)
B.RC=new A.f0("Recovery authenticates too","A recovery process makes the same consequential decision as the login process: whether a claimant gets control of the account. It may ask for different evidence, but it is not a side note. Select a route and let the executable system show what that route can replace.",null)
B.a7y=new A.He(null)
B.WL=s([B.RC,B.a7y],t.p)
B.RK=new A.on(10,"The other way into the account",u.W,B.WL,u.B,"/password/journey/defense-in-depth","Assemble the protection layers",null)
B.Rz=new A.f0("Hold the person constant","Assume the same person is willing to continue on either page. Change the page and the kind of factor. A one-time code can be copied from one conversation into another. A security key receives the page\u2019s real origin from the browser and signs only for the site where it was registered.",null)
B.a0Z=new A.GC(null)
