B.a2A=new A.dO("WannaCry and EternalBlue","WannaCry used the EternalBlue technique against vulnerable Windows file-sharing services and then propagated between reachable computers.","Organizations around the world lost access to systems and data; hospitals, businesses, and public services experienced severe operational disruption.","Exploit reliability, exposed services, patch state, and network reachability combined to turn one capability into widespread impact. Segmentation and verified patching could break that chain.")
B.a2H=new A.dO("The Target payment-card intrusion","Attackers entered through credentials connected to a third party and moved across internal systems until they could place malware in the payment environment.","Payment-card and customer information was stolen, creating financial, legal, operational, and reputational costs.","The first foothold was not the final objective. Access boundaries, credential scope, monitoring, and segmentation determine whether an initial capability can be chained into material harm.")
B.SC=s([B.a2A,B.a2H],t.AU)
B.CX=new A.hU("exploit","Real incidents demonstrate that an exploit is valuable because of the capability it creates and the systems that capability can reach next.",B.SC,null)
B.a2Q=new A.h0("Exploits in the Real World",B.CX,"/exploits",null)
B.a2N=new A.dO("Stuxnet","The operation combined several previously unknown Windows vulnerabilities with stolen trust and detailed knowledge of an industrial control process.","The malware crossed digital boundaries and contributed to physical damage to Iranian uranium-enrichment equipment while spreading beyond its intended environment.","An unknown flaw becomes much more consequential when it inherits trusted code, weak segmentation, portable media paths, and access to physical control.")
B.a2v=new A.dO("Pegasus zero-click compromises","Attack chains used previously unknown mobile-platform flaws to compromise targeted phones without requiring the recipient to click a link.","Successful compromise could expose private communications, location, sensors, and accounts, putting journalists, activists, officials, and their contacts at risk.","When prevention fails without a user mistake, isolation, rapid updates, behavioral evidence, restricted privileges, and response capacity become essential layers.")
B.U1=s([B.a2N,B.a2v],t.AU)
B.D_=new A.hU("zero-day","Zero-days matter because the gap between discovery and dependable remediation can produce consequences far beyond the vulnerable component.",B.U1,null)
B.a2R=new A.h0("Zero-Days in the Real World",B.D_,"/zero-days",null)
B.a2D=new A.dO("Heartbleed","A missing bounds check in a widely deployed OpenSSL feature allowed remote requests to read portions of process memory.","Potentially sensitive memory\u2014including credentials and cryptographic material\u2014could be exposed, requiring patching, investigation, certificate replacement, and credential resets.","Memory-safe design, boundary checks, focused testing, review, and layered secret handling reduce both the likelihood and consequence of one implementation error.")
B.a2L=new A.dO("Log4Shell","A logging library interpreted attacker-controlled text in a way that could trigger remote lookups and code execution in vulnerable configurations.","Organizations had to find deeply embedded copies across products and services, mitigate active exploitation, update dependencies, and investigate exposure under intense time pressure.","Untrusted data should remain data. Dependency inventories, safe defaults, constrained runtime authority, rapid updates, and behavioral monitoring are part of application security.")
B.Xj=s([B.a2D,B.a2L],t.AU)
B.CR=new A.hU("secure-coding","Software incidents show that small implementation choices can cross trust boundaries, expose data, and create emergency work throughout an ecosystem.",B.Xj,null)
B.a2S=new A.h0("Secure Coding in the Real World",B.CR,"/secure",null)
B.a2z=new A.dO("The DigiNotar certificate-authority compromise","Attackers compromised a trusted certificate authority and issued fraudulent certificates that could impersonate major online services.","Users could be directed through apparently valid encrypted connections controlled by an attacker; browsers removed trust in DigiNotar and the company collapsed.","Encryption without trustworthy identity validation can create a protected connection to the wrong party. Trust anchors need restriction, monitoring, revocation, and recovery.")
B.a2I=new A.dO("The Debian OpenSSL random-number failure","A software change drastically reduced the unpredictability of cryptographic keys generated on affected systems.","Keys created over an extended period became guessable and had to be identified, revoked, and replaced across many systems.","A respected algorithm cannot repair predictable key generation. Implementation review and a workable key-replacement lifecycle are part of cryptographic design.")
B.WU=s([B.a2z,B.a2I],t.AU)
B.CZ=new A.hU("encryption","Encryption failures rarely reduce to someone defeating the mathematics. Trust, randomness, keys, validation, implementation, and recovery determine the real protection.",B.WU,null)
B.a2T=new A.h0("Encryption in the Real World",B.CZ,"/encryption",null)
B.a2w=new A.dO("The Equifax breach","Attackers used a known Apache Struts vulnerability on an internet-facing application that had not been patched, then remained in the environment and accessed sensitive records.","Personal information belonging to roughly 147 million people was exposed, followed by years of identity risk, response costs, legal consequences, and loss of trust.","A published patch is not a control until teams know where the component exists, deploy the repair, verify it, monitor the exposure window, and limit access to sensitive data.")
B.a2C=new A.dO("Heartbleed","A bounds-checking flaw in a widely used OpenSSL feature allowed remote requests to read portions of server memory.","Systems faced possible exposure of passwords, session data, and private cryptographic material; remediation required patching plus certificate and credential replacement where exposure was plausible.","Removing the code flaw does not remove everything the flaw may have revealed. Complete remediation follows the consequences as well as the vulnerable component.")
B.Vn=s([B.a2w,B.a2C],t.AU)
B.CU=new A.hU("vulnerability","Major incidents show that a weakness causes harm through a chain: affected components must be present, reachable, exploitable, and connected to assets that matter.",B.Vn,null)
B.a2U=new A.h0("Vulnerabilities in the Real World",B.CU,"/osappholes",null)
B.a2V=new A.h0("Password Security in the Real World",B.CT,"/password",null)
B.a2K=new A.dO("The Capital One breach","An attacker abused a server-side request path and the cloud permissions available to the affected workload to obtain customer data.","Information associated with more than 100 million credit applications and accounts was exposed, followed by response costs, regulatory consequences, and loss of trust.","A perimeter control is only one layer. Workload identity, least privilege, metadata protections, storage access, monitoring, and configuration review must bound the capability created by a foothold.")
B.a2B=new A.dO("Internet-exposed MongoDB databases","Many database instances were reachable from the internet without effective authentication, allowing automated attackers to erase or copy data and leave ransom demands.","Organizations lost data, faced extortion, and discovered that a convenient default deployment had silently become public infrastructure.","Secure defaults, explicit network exposure, authentication, backups, and continuous configuration checks prevent convenience from becoming an unowned trust boundary.")
B.V4=s([B.a2K,B.a2B],t.AU)
B.CS=new A.hU("hardening","Hardening failures show how exposed features, excessive permissions, weak administration, and configuration drift combine into consequential attack paths.",B.V4,null)
B.a2W=new A.h0("System Hardening in the Real World",B.CS,"/hardening",null)
B.a2J=new A.dO("The Target payment-card intrusion","Attackers used third-party credentials, moved through internal systems, and placed malware in the payment environment while alerts and boundaries failed to stop the chain.","Payment-card and customer information was stolen, producing financial, legal, operational, leadership, and reputational consequences.","Initial access is only the first event. Vendor access, credential scope, segmentation, monitoring, escalation, and response ownership determine whether a foothold reaches critical assets.")
B.a2M=new A.dO("NotPetya at Maersk","Destructive malware entered through a trusted software-update path and spread rapidly through connected enterprise systems, damaging identity and operational infrastructure.","Global shipping operations were severely disrupted and thousands of systems had to be rebuilt, creating enormous operational and financial losses.","Trusted dependencies can become entry paths; segmentation, protected identity infrastructure, offline recovery, inventories, and practiced rebuilding determine whether an organization can restore its mission.")
B.WP=s([B.a2J,B.a2M],t.AU)
B.CY=new A.hU("capstone","Large incidents show why response decisions must connect identity, software, networks, operations, evidence, and recovery rather than treating each alert in isolation.",B.WP,null)
B.a2X=new A.h0("Integrated Incidents in the Real World",B.CY,"/capstone",null)
B.a2Y=new A.h0("Networking in the Real World",B.CW,"/networking",null)
B.a2x=new A.dO("The 2020 Twitter account takeover","Attackers socially engineered employees and gained access to internal account-support tools, then used those tools against prominent accounts.","Compromised accounts promoted a cryptocurrency scam, damaged confidence in a major communication platform, and exposed the power concentrated in internal support access.","Training alone cannot contain privileged tools. Strong identity checks, least privilege, approval boundaries, monitoring, and rapid reporting must limit what one persuaded employee can enable.")
B.a2G=new A.dO("A deepfake-assisted payment fraud","A finance employee joined a video meeting populated by convincing synthetic versions of familiar colleagues and followed instructions to transfer company funds.","The company lost millions, and realistic audio and video could no longer be treated as sufficient proof of identity or authority.","Independent contact routes and normal multi-person approval procedures remain useful even when an impersonation looks and sounds perfect.")
B.US=s([B.a2x,B.a2G],t.AU)
B.CQ=new A.hU("social-engineering","Social engineering succeeds when a convincing story can borrow legitimate authority and move through ordinary communication and approval systems.",B.US,null)
B.a2Z=new A.h0("Social Engineering in the Real World",B.CQ,"/social",null)
B.a2E=new A.dO("The Equifax breach","A fix for a critical Apache Struts vulnerability was available, but an affected internet-facing application remained vulnerable and was exploited.","Personal information belonging to roughly 147 million people was exposed, creating lasting identity risk, response costs, legal consequences, and loss of trust.","A patch notice and an internal request do not prove remediation. Asset ownership, deployment evidence, scanning, and escalation must close the loop.")
B.a2y=new A.dO("WannaCry","Ransomware spread through vulnerable Windows file-sharing services after Microsoft had released updates addressing the exploited weakness.","Organizations worldwide experienced encrypted systems and operational outages, including serious disruption to healthcare and other essential services.","Unsupported systems, incomplete coverage, exposed services, and flat networks can turn delayed patching into a rapidly propagating incident.")
B.U2=s([B.a2E,B.a2y],t.AU)
B.CV=new A.hU("patch","Patch failures show that release, prioritization, deployment, verification, and investigation are separate parts of one operational system.",B.U2,null)
B.a3_=new A.h0("Patch Management in the Real World",B.CV,"/patch",null)
