B.asM=new A.dR("visible","Can an observer read it?","Encryption addresses that question, assuming the key remains protected.",!1)
B.asT=new A.dR("changed","Was it altered before the recipient processed it?","Correct. Confidentiality alone does not necessarily detect tampering.",!0)
B.asA=new A.dR("stored","Can it be stored on a disk?","Storage is independent of proving integrity.",!1)
B.WM=s([B.asM,B.asT,B.asA],t.vS)
B.at5=new A.kh(5,"Secrecy is not the whole claim",u.Y,"Confidentiality, integrity, and identity differ","Encryption primarily provides confidentiality. Authenticated encryption can also detect modification. Digital signatures can bind a message to control of a private key, while certificates help connect a public key to an identity. Each guarantee requires an explicit mechanism and correct validation.","A payment instruction is encrypted, but the design provides no integrity check. What important question remains?",B.WM,u.u,"/encryption/journey/https-boundary","Build an HTTPS session",null,null)
B.asN=new A.dR("loss","The laptop can no longer be lost","Encryption cannot prevent physical loss.",!1)
B.asL=new A.dR("reading","The stored files can be read from the stolen drive","Correct. Encryption at rest makes copied storage unintelligible unless the thief also obtains the key.",!0)
B.asJ=new A.dR("deletion","The thief can no longer destroy the files","Encryption protects confidentiality, not availability. Backups address loss or destruction.",!1)
B.WW=s([B.asN,B.asL,B.asJ],t.vS)
B.at6=new A.kh(1,"Start with the exposure","What does encryption protect against?","Encryption narrows what possession means","Encryption transforms readable plaintext into ciphertext. Someone who copies the ciphertext should not be able to recover the plaintext without the key. It protects data from disclosure; it does not recover a device, prevent deletion, or stop an already-authorized user from reading it.","A thief steals a powered-off, fully encrypted laptop but does not know its password. Which risk has encryption reduced?",B.WW,u.X,"/encryption/journey/transformation","Transform the message",null,null)
B.asO=new A.dR("brand","Is the algorithm name well known?","A familiar algorithm is useful, but it does not assess the surrounding design.",!1)
B.asG=new A.dR("lifecycle","How are keys, validation, endpoints, and recovery handled?","Correct. The protection is only as strong as the complete lifecycle and boundary.",!0)
B.asQ=new A.dR("secret-code","Can the team keep its use of encryption secret?","A design should remain secure even when its methods are public.",!1)
B.Up=s([B.asO,B.asG,B.asQ],t.vS)
B.at7=new A.kh(8,"The algorithm is only one component",u.o,"Cryptography succeeds or fails as a system","Failures often come from predictable randomness, reused nonces, leaked keys, skipped certificate checks, unsafe recovery paths, obsolete settings, vulnerable endpoints, or implementations that reveal secrets. Sound design selects reviewed protocols and libraries, minimizes key access, rotates and revokes keys, validates identities, and plans recovery.","A team uses a respected encryption library. Which review question is still essential?",B.Up,"You can now ask the central design question: what data is protected, from whom, at which boundary, and who controls the keys?","/encryption/real-world","Review real-world outcomes",B.CZ,null)
B.asP=new A.dR("never","No; end-to-end encryption protects it everywhere","The message must become readable at an intended endpoint.",!1)
B.asR=new A.dR("endpoint","Yes; malware can capture plaintext at the endpoint","Correct. Strong transport protection does not make endpoints invulnerable.",!0)
B.asD=new A.dR("network","Only if the attacker also controls the network","Endpoint compromise can bypass the network protection entirely.",!1)
B.UH=s([B.asP,B.asR,B.asD],t.vS)
B.at8=new A.kh(7,"Name the protection boundary",u.k,"At rest, in transit, and end to end are different","Encryption at rest protects stored data when its storage is obtained without the key. Encryption in transit protects a network connection between particular endpoints. End-to-end encryption keeps intermediaries from holding message plaintext, but intended endpoints still decrypt it. Metadata, compromised endpoints, screenshots, and authorized sharing may remain outside the boundary.","A message is end-to-end encrypted, but the recipient\u2019s unlocked phone is compromised. Can the message still be exposed?",B.UH,u.o,"/encryption/journey/failures","Inspect the system failures",null,null)
B.au0=new A.fu("helpful","Whether the caller sounds knowledgeable and helpful","A convincing performance is part of the offer; it does not establish authorization.",!1)
B.au2=new A.fu("exchange","The hidden exchange: promised help for account access","This is quid pro quo. Verify support through an official route and never approve an unexpected sign-in.",!0)
B.atY=new A.fu("speed","Whether the fix will save enough time","A useful reward can still conceal an unsafe requested action.",!1)
B.Ve=s([B.au0,B.au2,B.atY],t.r5)
B.at9=new A.pE(5,"When the request looks like an offer",u.bE,"The exchange can hide the cost","Baiting offers something tempting\u2014such as a download, prize, or found USB drive\u2014to provoke an unsafe action. Quid pro quo promises help or a service in exchange for information or access. Instead of threatening a loss, both tactics make compliance appear rewarding.","A caller says they are fixing slow employee accounts and can improve yours immediately. They ask you to approve a sign-in prompt while they work. What matters most?",B.Ve,u.v,"/social/journey/physical-boundaries","Approach the door",null)
B.asE=new A.dR("honest","The operator is an honest bank","Certificates do not certify the operator\u2019s intentions.",!1)
B.asF=new A.dR("domain","The connection is protected to the displayed domain","Correct. The user must still verify that the domain is the intended one.",!0)
B.asI=new A.dR("device","The user\u2019s device contains no malware","HTTPS cannot assess the security of the endpoint.",!1)
B.Xu=s([B.asE,B.asF,B.asI],t.vS)
B.ata=new A.kh(6,"HTTPS protects a connection\u2014not every surrounding decision",u.u,"Certificate, handshake, session","The browser validates a certificate for the requested site, the handshake establishes fresh session keys, and authenticated encryption protects traffic in transit. HTTPS can show that the connection is to the named domain and resist interception. It does not prove the site is honest, prevent phishing, or secure either endpoint.","A convincing fake bank site has a valid HTTPS certificate for its own look-alike domain. What does the padlock establish?",B.Xu,u.k,"/encryption/journey/boundaries","Map the boundaries",null,null)
B.atV=new A.fu("hold","Hold the secure door; they look like a delivery worker","Appearance and a plausible story do not supply authorization.",!1)
B.atX=new A.fu("badge","Let them badge in themselves or escort them to reception","This preserves the access boundary while still offering legitimate help.",!0)
B.au4=new A.fu("ignore","Walk away without saying anything","That avoids granting access, but a clear, supported route to reception is safer and more helpful.",!1)
B.VE=s([B.atV,B.atX,B.au4],t.r5)
B.atb=new A.pE(6,"Politeness at a physical boundary",u.v,"Tailgating borrows another person\u2019s access","Tailgating occurs when an unauthorized person follows an authorized person into a restricted place. The attacker may carry boxes, wear familiar clothing, or claim a broken badge. The mechanism is familiar: create a situation in which enforcing the boundary feels awkward or unhelpful.","Someone carrying packages asks you to hold a badge-controlled door because their hands are full. What is the safest helpful response?",B.VE,u.bP,"/social/journey/synthetic-impersonation","Challenge the impersonation",null)
B.asC=new A.dR("safe","The backup remains safe because the algorithm is strong","The attacker has both required pieces, so algorithm strength does not preserve secrecy.",!1)
B.asz=new A.dR("lost","The key exposure defeats the backup\u2019s confidentiality","Correct. Separating and restricting the key is essential.",!0)
B.asS=new A.dR("corrupt","The backup becomes corrupted","Exposure makes reading possible; it does not inherently damage the data.",!1)
B.Wm=s([B.asC,B.asz,B.asS],t.vS)
B.atc=new A.kh(3,"Encryption moves the access decision to the key",u.cA,"Key custody is part of the security boundary","A strong algorithm cannot compensate for an exposed key. Systems must decide where keys are generated, stored, unlocked, rotated, backed up, and revoked. A device may be encrypted while powered off yet expose plaintext after a user unlocks it.","A database backup is strongly encrypted, but its decryption key is stored in the same publicly readable folder. What is the result?",B.Wm,u.aq,"/encryption/journey/hybrid","Compare key roles",null,null)
B.atW=new A.fu("retrain","Retrain the employee and wait for evidence of misuse","Training may help later, but waiting leaves active credentials and sessions exposed.",!1)
B.au1=new A.fu("contain","Revoke sessions, reset credentials, inspect activity, and preserve the report","This contains the incident, checks downstream effects, and turns the report into useful detection evidence.",!0)
B.au_=new A.fu("punish","Discipline the employee so others will be more careful","Punishment discourages rapid reporting\u2014the behavior the response process most needs.",!1)
B.Vy=s([B.atW,B.au1,B.au_],t.r5)
B.atd=new A.pE(8,"Design for mistakes and recovery",u.E,"Awareness is one layer, not the whole defense","Good systems reduce how often one hurried decision becomes a disaster. They combine clear verification routes, least privilege, phishing-resistant sign-in, approval limits, physical access controls, reporting that is easy and blame-free, rapid session revocation, and recovery procedures that attackers cannot casually redirect.","An employee entered credentials on a fake page and reports it immediately. Which organizational response is strongest?",B.Vy,u.x,"/social/journey/combined-scenario","Run the final scenario",null)
B.atZ=new A.fu("glitches","Look for blinking, lip-sync, or lighting mistakes","Those clues can help, but their absence cannot authenticate the caller.",!1)
B.atU=new A.fu("process","Use the normal approval process and a separately known contact route","Process-based verification does not depend on deciding whether the media looks real.",!0)
B.au3=new A.fu("personal","Ask for personal details the executive would know","Personal information may be public, stolen, or supplied to the impersonator.",!1)
B.WI=s([B.atZ,B.atU,B.au3],t.r5)
B.ate=new A.pE(7,"When a voice or face is not proof",u.bP,"Deepfakes strengthen impersonation, not authorization","Synthetic audio, video, and images can imitate a familiar person. Visual glitches may offer clues, but improving media makes clue-hunting unreliable. A recognizable voice or face should be treated as a claim. Authorization still needs an independent process.","A realistic video call from an executive requests an urgent payment outside the normal process. Which check remains useful even if the video is perfect?",B.WI,u.E,"/social/journey/design-for-recovery","Build the defenses",null)
B.asB=new A.dR("public-all","Use public-key operations for every byte forever","Possible in narrow cases, but unnecessarily costly for bulk traffic.",!1)
B.asH=new A.dR("hybrid","Authenticate and establish a session, then use a symmetric session key","Correct. The methods are complementary, not competing.",!0)
B.asK=new A.dR("publish-secret","Publish one shared secret so every browser can use it","A published shared secret is no longer secret.",!1)
B.UK=s([B.asB,B.asH,B.asK],t.vS)
B.atf=new A.kh(4,"Different keys solve different coordination problems",u.aq,"Modern protocols combine their strengths","Symmetric encryption uses a shared secret and efficiently protects large amounts of data. Public-key cryptography uses a public/private pair to establish trust, exchange secrets, or create signatures without first sharing one secret privately. Protocols such as HTTPS commonly use public-key methods to establish a session, then symmetric keys for the traffic.","A browser begins a secure session with a server it has never contacted. Which design best fits the two jobs?",B.UK,u.Y,"/encryption/journey/integrity","Separate the guarantees",null,null)
